Think of a Python decorator as a high-tech exoskeleton for your functions. It is a function that takes another function as its input, 
straps on additional capabilities—like automated retries, execution timing, or logging—and returns a heavily upgraded version, 
all without changing a single line of the original function's internal code.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Because functions in Python are 'first-class citizens', they can be passed around as arguments just like strings or integers.
A decorator leverages this by acting as a wrapper. When you place the @ symbol (like @timer) directly above a function definition, 
Python intercepts the execution. Instead of running the raw function, the interpreter runs the decorator first.
The decorator executes its own pre-processing logic, triggers the original function from within itself (via a closure),
captures the result, executes any post-processing logic, and finally returns the output.

Buisness Context :-
From a business perspective, decorators enforce the DRY principle—Don't Repeat Yourself—at an enterprise scale. 
If you have 50 different data extraction scripts and the business mandates that all pipeline durations must be tracked for cloud cost optimization, you don't copy-paste timing logic 
into 50 different files. You write one @track_duration decorator and stamp it across the codebase, standardizing observability instantly with zero risk of breaking the core logic.

Design Decision :- 
engineers often avoid writing custom decorators because the nested *args and **kwargs syntax looks intimidating, resulting in spaghetti code bloated with repetitive try-except blocks.
As a data engineer, I use decorators aggressively to build resilient pipelines. I wrap volatile third-party API calls in a @retry_with_exponential_backoff decorator to silently
handle transient network drops. Furthermore, mastering this is mandatory for modern data engineering: Apache Airflow’s TaskFlow API relies entirely on the @task decorator 
to magically convert standard Python functions into distributed DAG nodes, completely hiding the complex XCom state-passing mechanics under the hood.
